# What the document says its remainder is, beside the one the arithmetic derives. One row per
# layer that both files a remainder and can have one computed from its own `n - d`, carrying both
# figures so a caller can set them against each other.
#
# One pair, three questions
#   This is a relation somebody in this domain would name. `arithmetic/filed_remainder.sqlc`,
#   `matrices.sqlc` and `queries/matrices/4-converted-remainder.sqlc` each need
#   `filed_remainders` joined to `remainder` on (filing, layer), for three different questions
#   about the same pair of numbers. The pair is the object; the questions are the callers'.
#
# What the inner join drops
#   It is an inner join, and the rows it drops are two different kinds of absence. A layer that
#   files no remainder at all is not here (layers/filed_remainders.sqlc keeps
#   `remainder_absent IS NULL`), and neither is one whose demand or nameplate is unstated, so
#   nothing could be derived. Both are honest and both are somebody else's question:
#   epistemics/absences.sqlc holds the first, arithmetic/remainder.sqlc the second.
#
# The derived figure is the remainder in force
#   `r` and its magnitude come from layers/remainder.sqlc: the pivoted `F Φ r - e_n + e_d` on a
#   composed layer that owes an exact remainder, the crossed `n - d` everywhere else. So a stated
#   quantity is compared with the remainder the model actually holds, and
#   checks/stated_quantity_is_not_the_magnitude can require them equal.
#
# The composed totals are carried too
#   `d_*` and `n_*` are the layer's own composed totals. Where a conversion factor multiplies both
#   the nameplate and the demand of a part, the two converted totals are correlated, and
#   differencing them as if independent counts the factor's spread twice.
#   `queries/matrices/4-converted-remainder.sqlc` re-derives that crossed difference from these
#   columns to show where it departs from the remainder: equal at the mode, wider at both bounds.
-- layers/filed_remainders.sqlc against layers/remainder.sqlc, on the layer they share.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.qty_low, l.qty_mode, l.qty_high, l.qty_unit, l.qty_absent,
       r.r_low, r.r_mode, r.r_high, r.unit,
       r.m_low, r.m_mode, r.m_high,
       r.d_low, r.d_mode, r.d_high,
       r.n_low, r.n_mode, r.n_high, r.amount_unit,
       r.sign, r.derived_fit
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/filed_remainders.sqlc)
) l
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc)
) r USING (filing, layer)
